    "slot_key": "Course roster, assignment, submission, and grade records",
    "slot_value": "A course is the overall class offering; a section is a specific instance of that course with its own instructor, schedule, and roster; assignments are anything students are expected to submit or complete for credit (paper, quiz, project, or discussion participation); grades reflect the instructor's evaluation of student performance in that section; feedback is comments or guidance tied to a submission or performance. Instructors should be able to give feedback before grading, after grading, or at the same time. Students should be able to view feedback for their own work, but not other students' unless the instructor explicitly posts it for the class. If an instructor posts feedback by mistake, they should be able to edit it, and changes should be recorded for accountability. Students should be able to view course content, submit assignments, participate in discussions, and see their own grades and feedback. Professors should be able to create and manage course materials, assignments, discussions, and grading for the courses they teach. Enrollment should control access tightly so only officially enrolled students can use the full course tools; waitlisted students should get only temporary limited access during add/drop, such as basic course information and possibly instructor-approved materials, and dropped students should lose access quickly with a short grace period if needed for already-submitted assignments or pending administrative updates. Waitlisted students should not have full participation rights unless they are actually enrolled. The system should follow the official enrollment status, with enough flexibility to avoid disrupting legitimate transitions. Special cases like auditors or accommodations require explicit rules, including exception-based access for approved auditors or disability accommodations, with access limited to the specific materials or tools needed rather than full course access by default, and removed promptly when approval expires or the accommodation changes. For cross-listed courses, the roster should be one shared course roster with the student's section tracked behind the scenes as authoritative enrollment plus section membership, so enrollment changes or section changes update that section assignment without creating duplicate enrollment records.",
    "operation": "refine"
  }
]

